# Review Panel

## Overview

The correctness core uses reviewers with opposite instructions. The semantic reviewer checks deep
behavior and contracts. The mechanical reviewer checks every changed hunk for local defects. The
`stacked` mode adds a second semantic reviewer from another model family.

Correctness review still assumes that the chosen design is right. Optional design and security seats
apply different standards. The design seat judges the change against its goal or design principles.
The security seat judges it against the trust model.

**Core principle: give each reviewer one standard. Do not combine sharp semantic review, exhaustive
mechanical review, design challenge, and security analysis in one prompt.**

## Explicit-only

Invoke this skill by name with `/review-panel`. It does not trigger from a generic "review this"
request. The deprecated `/two-pass-review` command loads this workflow for compatibility. When Pi
Flow is active, `reviewing-change-sets` is the review orchestrator. Use that flow instead of this
skill, and do not run both orchestrators at the same checkpoint.

## When to use

- Before requesting human review or merging a diff or pull request, when you explicitly want this
  review structure.
- After a plan or specification, before implementation.
- When a prior review surfaced a class of issue that another reviewer missed.
- Not for trivial diffs such as a one-line documentation change.

## Modes

The mode selects the correctness core. The optional seats compose with either mode.

- `--mode layered` **(default)** selects one semantic reviewer and one mechanical reviewer. Two
  correctness seats.
- `--mode stacked` **(explicit only)** selects the two-reviewer semantic panel from the
  `dual-adversarial-review` skill, plus the mechanical reviewer. Three correctness seats. Reserve it
  for high-risk changes and only use it when the caller accepts the added cost.
- `--design` adds one design and architecture reviewer. Ask the caller for the change's goal or
  intent, such as a design document or ticket. If none exists, record that fact and use the fallback
  design principles in the design prompt. The seat must not infer intent from the as-built contract.
- `--security` adds one security reviewer. It is off by default and runs only when the caller asks
  for it.

Examples include `--mode layered --design`, `--mode stacked --security`, and
`--mode layered --design --security`.

The mechanical pass runs identically in both modes. The optional seats also keep the same mandate in
both modes.

### Compatibility aliases

- `--mode single` maps to `layered`.
- `--mode dual` is ambiguous and no longer runs. The old `dual` silently added a third seat. Select
  `stacked` explicitly for two semantic reviewers plus the mechanical reviewer. Otherwise, use
  `layered`.

The `layered` and `stacked` names match the corresponding Pi Flow review shapes. This compatibility
note does not make this skill part of Pi Flow.

## Process

1. **Identify the review inputs.** Collect the working diff, pull request, commit range, or plan file.
   Include the repository root, relevant conventions, approved requirements, and raw evidence.
2. **Collect design intent when requested.** For `--design`, ask for the stated goal or intent. Prefer
   a design document or ticket. A written statement from the caller is enough. If none exists, tell
   the caller that the seat can judge structure against the fallback principles but cannot decide
   whether the change solves the right product problem.
3. **Prepare independent reviewer packets.** Give each reviewer raw evidence and only the standard it
   needs:
   - Semantic reviewers receive the approved behavior contract and relevant code.
   - The mechanical reviewer receives every changed file and the named conventions.
   - The design reviewer receives the goal or intent and the change. Do not substitute the as-built
     contract for the goal. If no intent exists, provide the fallback principles and state that the
     goal is unknown.
   - The security reviewer receives the change, trust boundaries, actors, permissions, data classes,
     and deployment context that are known. Mark missing trust-model facts instead of inventing them.
   Never include another reviewer's verdict in a reviewer packet.
4. **Dispatch every selected seat in one parallel batch.** Do not run a later seat after reading an
   earlier verdict.
   - For `layered`, dispatch one Opus semantic reviewer with the **Semantic prompt**.
   - For `stacked`, dispatch the independent Claude Opus and Codex semantic reviewers defined by
     `dual-adversarial-review`, using the **Semantic prompt** and the same raw evidence.
   - Dispatch one Sonnet mechanical reviewer with the **Mechanical checklist**.
   - If `--design` is present, dispatch one Opus design reviewer with the **Design seat prompt**.
   - If `--security` is present, dispatch one Opus security reviewer with the **Security seat
     prompt**.
5. **Verify and synthesize.** Check every claim against the artifact and code. Remove false positives
   and duplicates within each review category. Keep the result categories separate as described in
   **Synthesis**.
6. **Report only.** This skill does not implement fixes. Hand accepted changes to the normal edit
   flow.

## Semantic prompt

> Adversarial semantic reviewer. Read `<artifact>` and verify every claim against the real code.
> Name the key files. Find correctness bugs, cross-path or contract inconsistencies, atomicity or
> idempotency gaps, concurrency or time-of-check/time-of-use problems, API and HTTP status semantics,
> and weak tests. Weak tests include tests that pass when the code is broken and missing negative or
> boundary cases. Do not hunt mechanical nits. A separate reviewer owns null checks, casts, unused
> dependencies, documentation errors, and dead code. Keep the signal high. For each finding, report
> severity `[BLOCKER|MAJOR|MINOR]`, location, why the behavior is wrong, and a concrete fix. Review
> only. Do not implement.

## Mechanical checklist

> Mechanical reviewer. Walk every changed hunk. Report every local defect. Noise is acceptable
> because exhaustiveness is the goal. Emit a verdict for every changed file, including `clean`, so
> no file is skipped. Apply each lens below.

| Lens | Ask of every changed hunk |
|---|---|
| Null safety | Can a new field, parameter, or return value be null? Is each dereference guarded? Are maps and collections copied and non-null where required? |
| Cast and type | Is each narrow cast safe for driver or decoded data? Are there unchecked or raw generic types? |
| Dependencies | Is each new build dependency imported and used? Are any imports unused? |
| Documentation and code | Does each nearby comment, API description, and documentation claim still match the code? |
| Quantified claims | Prove changed claims that use words such as `exactly`, `every`, `only`, `always`, or `never`. Do not trust a claim that a heuristic catches all unsafe cases. |
| Generated files and configuration | Is each OpenAPI, JSON Schema, and YAML value valid for its type? Are generated files synchronized? Are constraints documented? |
| Dead code | Does an annotation suppress nothing? Is a branch unreachable? Is a field, method, or parameter unused? |
| Messages | Does each exception, log, or error message match the condition that produces it? |
| Interpolated strings | Does untrusted input enter a message template, log format, expression language, SQL, Cypher, or JPQL string? Require escaping or parameter binding. |
| Conventions | Does the change follow the named repository conventions for naming, tests, and formatting? |

> For each finding, report severity, location, a one-line defect, and a concrete fix. Review only. Do
> not implement.

## Design seat prompt

> Design and architecture reviewer. Your standard is `<goal-or-intent>`, not the as-built behavior
> contract. If no goal or intent exists, say so and use this fallback standard: apply SOLID software
> design principles and Don't Repeat Yourself (DRY); prefer composition for API objects; use
> inheritance only when the types have a genuine substitutable API relationship and an interface is
> the right contract. Assume the code works. Do not hunt correctness bugs or mechanical nits because
> other reviewers own them.
>
> Challenge the design. Ask whether the public API is minimal and uses the right abstraction.
> Examine whether responsibilities are split correctly and live in the right layer or module. Find
> implementation details that leak to callers but should stay opaque. Question whether the contract
> itself is wrong. Identify choices that a later phase will have to undo or regret.
>
> For each finding, report the concern, location, why it matters for the goal or fallback standard,
> a concrete alternative shape, and one design severity: `[IRREVERSIBLE]` for a choice that cannot
> reasonably change later, `[EXPENSIVE-LATER]` for a choice whose change cost will grow, or
> `[PREFERENCE]` for a lower-cost design preference. Review only. Do not implement.

## Security seat prompt

> Security reviewer. Judge `<artifact>` against the trust model. Examine trust boundaries,
> authorization and permissions, SQL/Cypher/log/template/expression-language injection, data exposed
> in responses and logs, secret handling, server-side request forgery, multi-tenant isolation, and
> abuse or denial-of-service paths. State any trust-model facts that are missing. Do not use another
> reviewer's conclusions.
>
> For each finding, report severity `[CRITICAL|HIGH|MEDIUM|LOW]`, location, the exploit path, and a
> concrete fix. Review only. Do not implement.

## Synthesis

Report findings in separate labelled sections:

1. **Correctness findings.** Merge and deduplicate semantic and mechanical findings. Verify each
   finding. Order them by `[BLOCKER|MAJOR|MINOR]`. Note when multiple correctness reviewers found the
   same issue.
2. **Design and architecture findings.** Report design findings with their design severity and
   alternative shape. State whether the reviewer used explicit intent or fallback principles.
3. **Security findings.** Report security findings with security severity, exploit path, and fix.
4. **Shared seams.** Note where a design finding and a correctness finding concern the same API,
   boundary, layer, or contract. Cross-reference the findings without merging them.

Treat design and security findings as separate judgments, not correctness defects. Do not include
them in the correctness defect table or translate their severity into correctness severity. A shared
location does not make the findings duplicates because they answer different questions.

## Model & cost

| Selection | Seats | Model tier | Cost guidance |
|---|---:|---|---|
| `--mode layered` | 2 | Opus semantic, Sonnet mechanical | Default correctness core. |
| `--mode stacked` | 3 | Opus and Codex semantic, Sonnet mechanical | Higher cost. Use only when explicitly selected. |
| `--design` | +1 | Opus, same reasoning tier as semantic | Optional. Adds architecture analysis. |
| `--security` | +1 | Opus, same reasoning tier as semantic | Optional and off by default. Adds trust-model analysis. |

All selected seats run concurrently. Subagents return summaries only. The orchestrator reports the
verified synthesis, not raw reviews.

## Common mistakes

| Mistake | Fix |
|---|---|
| Combining several seats in one prompt | Give each seat one standard and one independent prompt. |
| Running optional seats after reading correctness findings | Dispatch every selected seat in one parallel batch. |
| Giving the design seat only the as-built contract | Supply the goal or intent. If none exists, state that and use the fallback principles. |
| Treating fallback principles as product intent | State that the seat can judge structure but cannot know whether the product goal is right. |
| Asking the design seat for bugs or nits | Tell it to assume the code works and challenge only the design. |
| Running security review by default | Add the seat only when the caller selects `--security`. |
| Blending all severities into one defect table | Keep correctness, design, and security sections separate. Cross-reference shared seams. |
| Giving one seat another seat's verdict | Give every seat its standard and raw evidence only. |
| No per-file verdict in the mechanical pass | Require a verdict for every changed file. |
| Accepting unverified findings | Check every claim against the artifact and code before reporting it. |
| Treating `stacked` as routine | Use it only when the caller explicitly accepts the third correctness seat and its cost. |

## Why the seats stay separate

The semantic and mechanical passes find different correctness failures. The design seat asks whether
the chosen shape is worth keeping even when it works. The security seat asks how an attacker or
untrusted tenant can cross a boundary. Separate prompts preserve those different standards. Separate
report sections keep their conclusions understandable.
